  :receipt/date "2026-08-18"
  :source/dataset "receipt"
  :receipt/title "Four builds repaired, and the one that must stay broken"
  :receipt/body
  "`verify-shadow-source-paths` reported 13 builds that cannot compile. Four are
   now repaired and verified by their own build; the count is 13 -> 9.

   ## The one that matters most

   `kotoba-lang/kura-conformance` is a LIVE CONFORMANCE SUITE, and its header
   says exactly why it exists: two bugs got past the unit suite in a row, both
   of the same shape — a synchronous IShardStore protocol reported every read as
   absent when handed an async transport, because FakeHttp in the tests was
   synchronous too. *A test that supplies its own world agrees with itself.*
   Only a suite running the real code against a real R2 bucket finds it.

   **It has been unable to build since 2026-07-29** — nearly three weeks. The
   net built to catch self-agreeing tests was itself silent, for the reason this
   whole class turns on: shadow-cljs stops at `The required namespace is not
   available` BEFORE any test runs, so the build is quiet rather than red.

   ## Repaired

     kura-conformance   +2 paths   76 files,  32 compiled
     kura-coordinator   +2 paths   79 files,  35 compiled
     kaigi/worker       +2 paths   69 files,  26 compiled
     gftdcojp/tia       +9 paths  159 files, 114 compiled, verify-bundle PASS 714 KB

   Each verified by running the repo's OWN build, not just the compile step —
   tia's script also runs its bundle verifier, which checks the `.run` and
   `fetch` exports survive `:advanced` renaming.

   All four pins advanced (pure fast-forwards, ahead 2 / behind 0 each) and the
   checkouts synced, so the fix is reachable rather than merely committed.

   ## Which ones were safe to touch, and why that had to be checked first

   `kura-conformance`, `kura-coordinator` and `kaigi` declare their own Worker
   names with NO routes; `tia` is `ai-gftd-tia` on `tia.gftd.ai/*`, unique. None
   can reassign a hostname.

   **Two are deliberately left broken.** Both
   `kotoba-lang/kotobase-protocols-worker` and
   `net-kotobase-enterprise-wave2/protocols-worker` declare the name
   `kotobase-protocols-worker`, which a LIVE Worker already answers to, and the
   enterprise-wave2 copy claims seven hostnames including
   `cypher.kotobase.net` — served today by a different Worker. Their broken
   builds are currently the only thing preventing a hostname reassignment.
   Repairing them arms it. See ADR-2608181700's :do-not.

   ## The detector was right, and not complete

   For tia it printed seven paths; adding them revealed `kotoba.kir`, and that
   revealed `kotoba.hir` — nine in total. The walk-through-misses logic reduces
   the rounds but does not always give the whole set in one pass: a path can
   only pull in files once it is present, and those files have their own
   requires. Two rounds instead of nine is the honest claim, not one.

   ## Newly visible, not newly caused

   kaigi's build now surfaces 6 pre-existing `:infer-warning` in
   `kaigi/worker/room.cljs`, unreachable while the build could not start. Left
   alone, but named: an un-inferred property read renamed by `:advanced`
   returning undefined is one of the two bugs kura-conformance exists to catch.

   ## Remaining 9

   Two duplicate pairs needing a canonical decision before repair (etzhayyim
   explorer x2 sharing `kotodama-explorer`; the aozora engines x3), one
   `MISSING` (kotobase-browser-worker declares `../kotobase-cljc-worker/src`,
   whose parent exists but whose src/ does not), one club-shinshi appview
   library, and the two protocols-worker copies that must stay as they are."
